l edge with the white rook on e7 dominating the open file.\n\nI.b. 1. white rook b1-d1 black rook e4-e5 2. white rook h3-g3 black rook e5-d5 3. white rook d1 takes black rook d5 black pawn c5-c4 4. white rook d5-d7. White defends the g-pawn with the rook, but Black activates on the d-file. After the trade, White's remaining rook reaches the seventh rank, though the position is slightly less favorable than after the direct invasion.\n\nI.c. 1. white rook b1-d1 black rook e4-e5 2. white rook h3-d3 black rook e5 takes white pawn g5 3. white rook d3-d7 black rook g5-h5 4. white rook d7 takes black pawn e7 black rook h5-h4. The rook centralizes on the d-file, but Black's check on h4 gains a tempo and the position is less favorable for White than after the direct seventh-rank invasion.\n\nII. white rook b1-f1 \u2014 a solid alternative that keeps the rook on the back rank and prepares a swing to the d-file. Black's best reply is 1... black pawn f7-f6, challenging the white pawn on g5 and opening the f-file for the rook. After 2. white rook f1-d1 black rook e4-g4 3. white rook d1-d7, White still seizes the seventh rank, but the extra tempo Black gains from the pawn break makes this slightly less favorable than the direct Rd1.\n\nIII. white rook h3-f3 \u2014 the least effective candidate. The rook leaves the open h-file and the white rook on b1 remains passive. Black's best is 1... black rook e4-h4, swinging to the h-file to target the white pawn on g5 and threaten checks along the fourth rank. After 2. white king h2-g3 black rook h4-d4 3. white rook b1-f1, White consolidates, but Black retains more active rook play than in the other lines.\n\nThe direct seventh-rank invasion via I is superior because it immediately creates a concrete threat against the black pawn on e7, forces Black into a reactive posture, and yields the most favorable resulting endgame.\n\nBEST_MOVE: white rook b1-d1\nCRITICAL_LINE: white rook b1-d1 black rook e4-e5 white rook d1-d7 black rook e5 takes w